      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#0 · Draft 3's positive appointment of finitude",
          "title": "An eye opens upon a world",
          "italic": "The whole begins with a capacity before any loss: the eye's condition does not arrive as one more face or obstacle — it is already at work in their appearing.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "An eye opens upon a world. Something stands forward; something recedes; a face can be approached, a path followed. There is a direction from which these things appear, even when attention belongs wholly to what appears. The eye's condition does not ordinarily arrive as one more object; it is already at work in their appearing. Turn towards that condition: a mirror shows the eye, a diagram describes its structures, another observer supplies an angle — each disclosure real, each occurring through a further",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "situation of seeing. The blindside in this authored image is more than the anatomical blind spot: it is the condition that lets a view have a foreground at all. The frame gives this condition an edge — its exclusion productive, since without an aperture there is no particular photograph. The horizon differs from the frame: the frame bounds a presentation; the horizon concerns the field within which something can come forward at all — walking changes it, another person's reply changes what the situation asks.\n\nOccurrences: the standing-forward and its direction (#0); the mirror and diagram disclosures (#0, deepened at #2's crystal); frame vs horizon (#0, usurped at #2).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
